Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT or THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT A. Francisco Gold Condominium II, EDSA cor. Mapagrnahal St., Diliman, Quezon City OFFICE OF SECRETARY OPINION NO. 29 S. 09 June 2005 VICE MAYOR ARLENE ARCILLAS-NAZARENO Santa Rosa City, Laguna Dear Vice Mayor Nazareno: This refers to your letter seeking our advice on what you should do in view of the inaction of Qovemor Teresita S. Lazaro of Laguna to sign the appointment papers of Mr. Roy Qonzales to fill up the vacancy in the Sangguniang Panlungsod of that city. You stated that with the untimely demise of your father, the late Mayor Leon C. Arcillas, and your subsequent assumption to the position of Vice Mayor in a permanent capacity being the most Senior City Councilor, the Sangguniang Panlungsod needs to fill up a vacant position created by your succession. You further stated that since you belong to the Lakas-CMD party, Honorable Jose de Venecia has already issued a nomination for the appointment of Mr. Roy Gonzales to fill up the vacancy pursuant to Section 45 of the Local Qovernment Code. Said nomination was then submitted to Qovernor Teresita S. Lazaro of Laguna for the issuance of the appointment but that despite several representations with Qovernor Lazaro, she has yet to sign the appointment papers of Mr. Qonzales as of the date of your writing. It is significant to note that in your letter, you stated that you are of the belief that once the documentary requirements are complied with, the issuance of the appointment becomes ministerial on the part of the appointing authority. In reply thereto, may we invite your attention to Section 45 (a) and (b) of the Local Government Code (RA 7160), which we quote: "SEC. 45. Permanent Vacancies in the Sanggunian.- (a) Permanent vacancies in the sanggunian where automatic successions provided above do not apply, shatt be fitted by appointment in the following manners: I. The President, through the Executive Secretary, in the case of the sangguniang pan Zatawigan and the sangguniang pantungsod Of highly urbanized cities and independent component cities;

-2- 2. The governor, in the case of the sangguniang panZungsod of component cities and the sangguniang bayan; 3. The city or municipal mayor, in the case of sangguniang barangay, upon recommendation of the sangguniang barangay concerned. Except for the sangguniang barangay, only the nominee of the political party under which the sanggunian member concerned had been elected and whose elevation to the position next higher in rank created the Zast vacancy in the sanggunian shall be appointed in the manner hereinabove provided. The appointee shall come from the same political party as that of the sanggunian member who caused the vacancy and shall serve the unexpired term of the vacant office. In the appointment herein mentioned, a nomination and a certificate of membership of the appointee from the highest official of the political party concerned are conditions sine qua non, and any appointment without such nomination and certification shall be null and void gu initio and shall be a ground for administrative action against the official responsible therefor. Hence, based on Section 45 (a) (2), since Sta. Rosa is a component city, then the appointing authority to fill up the vacancy is the Qovemor. With respect to the issue on which political party should issue the nomination required under Section 45 (b) of the Local Qovemment Code, the Supreme Court, in the leading case of Navarro vs. Court of Appeals (Q.R. No. 141397, 28 March 2001) which dealt on the proper interpretation of party nomination required in said Section 45 (b), ruled and has explained in detail that in case of death of the Mayor, the "Zast vacancy" is neither the vacancy caused by the elevation of the Vice Mayor to the Office of the Mayor nor the vacancy left by the last ranking sanggunian member when he was elevated to the 7 "1 rank position, but rather it is that vacancy caused by the elevation of the ISt ranking sanggunian member to the next higher rank, i.e., the Vice Mayor, by of succession. The Supreme Court ratiocinated that the reason behind the right given to a political pzuty to nominate a replacement where a permanent vacancy occurs in the sanggunian is to maintain the party representation as willed by the people in the election. This Department believes that in electoral democracy, conscientious people may choose candidates not only because of their personal attributes and qualifications but also because of their political party affiliation. Accordingly, in the Navarro case, the political party nomination should be issued by the political party of the IS ranking sanggunian member because he was the one who caused the last vacancy in the sanggunian due to his elevation to the position of Vice Mayor. It is noted that your case falls squarely with the case of Navarro vs.

-3- Court of Appeals. Hence, we find the nomination made by the Lakas-CMD to be in accordance with law and jurisprudence, it being your political pafty when you were elected then as ISt ranking Sangguniang Panlungsod Member. Along that line, it is a generally accepted principle that an appointing authority, in this case the Governor, enjoys a wide latitude of discretion. In the case of rarifias vs. Barba (G.R. No. 116763), the Supreme Court, in restating Section 45 (b) of the Local Government Code, ruled that the nomination and the certificate of membership of the appointee from the highest official of the political party concerned are conditions sine qua non, and any appointment without such nomination and certification shall be null and void ab initio and shall be a ground for administrative action against the official responsible therefor. In applying the principle that the appointing authority enjoys a wide latitude of discretion, vis-å-vis, Section 45 of the Local Government Code, it is clear that the discretion of the appointing authority lies in the determination as to whether or not the nominee to be appointed by him possesses all the required qualifications for an elective local official under Section 39 of the Local Government Code, and none of the disqualifications under Section 40 of the same Code and other existing laws, such that, once the appointing authority determines that the nominee possesses all the required qualifications and none cf the disqualifications for the position, it becomes a ministerial function on his part to issue the appointment. If it be otherwise, he may refuse to issue the same. Corollarily, under KA 6713, otherwise known as "The Code of Conduct and Ethical Standards for Public Officials and Employees", a public official is required to act on any request or matter brought before him within fifteen (15) days from receipt thereof. If the 15- day period provided for under RA 6713 has already lapsed, then action can now be demanded from the Qovemor to act as a matter of duty under the law. If the Qovemor refuses to sign the appointment despite compliance with the requirements set forth by law for the appointment of Mr. Qonzales, the remedy that you may avail yourself of is the filing of a civil case of Mandamus under Rule 65 of the Rules of Court in order to compel the Governor to act on a pending matter or request before her. We hope to have enlightened you on the matter. Legal:43/IA cc: Director Leonilo B. Lariosa DILG Region IV-A Calderon Bldg., EDSA Diliman, Quezon City Very truly yours, ANGELO T. REYES Secre tar-v OF TYE AMO LOCAL [NREPLYING SILG05-006261
